A spokesperson for Messina Touring Group told Rolling Stone on Monday (Sept. 14) that &#8220;as the concert promoter for Ed Sheeran’s U.S. Loop Tour, we have been notified by venues on the upcoming U.S. tour dates that they will not allow a concert to take place with Macklemore on the lineup.&#8221;

As this would apparently &#8220;result in the cancellation of the tour and impact hundreds of thousands of fans,&#8221; the team said. &#8220;After discussions with stakeholders, Macklemore will not be performing on the remaining support dates.&#8221;

Billboard has reached out to reps for Macklemore and Sheeran for comment.

The development comes less than two weeks after the &#8220;Thrift Shop&#8221; artist sparked controversy on the first night of his now-scrapped opening stint by saying on stage at MetLife Stadium in New Jersey, “A big part of the reason why I wanted to do this tour in the first place is so I could stand up in stadiums like this and say two words that are very near and dear to my heart: Free Palestine.&#8221;

&#8220;I want those words to be loud and clear, so that people all the way in Gaza to the occupied West Bank know that we have not forgotten about them,&#8221; he&#8217;d continued.

Macklemore has long been one of the most vocal artists in his support of Palestinians, dropping protest song &#8220;Hind&#8217;s Hall&#8221; &#8212; which he performed on stage before Sheeran&#8217;s set at MetLife &#8212; in 2024. On the track, he spits, “Where does genocide land in your definition, huh/ Destroyin’ every college in Gaza and every mosque/ Pushin’ everyone into Rafah and droppin’ bombs &#8230; We see the lies in ’em/ Claimin’ it’s antisemitic to be anti-Zionist/ I’ve seen Jewish brothers and sisters out there and ridin’ in/ Solidarity and screamin’ free Palestine with them.&#8221;

After his &#8220;free Palestine&#8221; plea on Sheeran&#8217;s tour, the Israeli-American Council published a petition to remove Macklemore from the rest of the U.S. leg. Pink also reshared a post calling for the rapper to be dropped from the trek and explained her point of view shortly afterward when people started criticizing her for it.

&#8220;Last weekend [Macklemore] stood in front of a stadium full of music-loving families, with violent images from a war on the screens, dedicated a song to the movement of his choice, and then took the mic to tell all of the Jews in the stadium that it wasn’t about them,&#8221; she wrote. &#8220;You don’t get to decide that for us.”

But as Macklemore stated on his second night opening for Sheeran at MetLife, “To all of my Jewish brothers and sisters: Criticism of Israel, criticism of apartheid, being against genocide in no way is a criticism of you.&#8221;

“Wanting all humans to be treated equal should never be controversial,” he also wrote on X a couple of days later.

The next Loop Tour show will take place Saturday (Sept. 19) in Philadelphia, featuring Lukas Graham and
Aaron Rowe, who were Macklemore&#8217;s co-openers. Sheeran has dates planned in the U.S. through Nov. 7, after which he&#8217;ll travel through Latin America with Finneas as opener through mid-December.
